WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.900
A mouse cone is a light-detecting cell of the retina.

2
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.260
Its sensitivity resets as the background light brightens.

3
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.160
That reset is called adaptation.

4
00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:22.080
The textbook account rests on two calcium sensors, the GCAPs and recoverin.

5
00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:25.720
Delete both, and the cone still adapts.

6
00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:27.580
So what is doing the work?

7
00:00:28.433 --> 00:00:33.773
Light closes channels in the outer segment, so calcium stops entering.

8
00:00:34.513 --> 00:00:38.193
A pump keeps removing calcium, so calcium falls.

9
00:00:39.213 --> 00:00:45.433
At low calcium, the GCAPs speed cGMP synthesis, which reopens channels.

10
00:00:46.693 --> 00:00:51.853
Recoverin lets go of GRK1, the kinase that switches off activated pigment.

11
00:00:52.653 --> 00:00:57.013
Both push the cell back toward its dark state while the light stays on.

12
00:00:57.013 --> 00:00:59.733
That push is gain control.

13
00:01:00.567 --> 00:01:02.967
To test a loop, cut it.

14
00:01:03.667 --> 00:01:07.187
Vinberg and Kefalov recorded from isolated mouse retina.

15
00:01:08.027 --> 00:01:12.247
The mice also lacked rod transducin, so the signal came from cones.

16
00:01:13.227 --> 00:01:18.987
They compared control cones with cones lacking GCAP1, GCAP2 and recoverin.

17
00:01:19.727 --> 00:01:26.467
The knockout cones still adapted, even with bath calcium lowered to an estimated 30 nM.

19
00:01:26.917 --> 00:01:32.157
Sensitivity is response size divided by the strength of a dim flash.

20
00:01:32.817 --> 00:01:38.277
It falls as the background brightens, and a fit with two numbers describes the fall.

21
00:01:39.477 --> 00:01:43.957
I0 is the background that halves sensitivity, and n is the slope.

22
00:01:45.497 --> 00:01:52.577
Control cones had I0 of 48,800 photons per square micrometre per second, and n of

23
00:01:52.577 --> 00:01:58.757
1. Cones lacking the sensors had 12,100 and n of 1.5.

24
00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:06.080
At a background of 48,800, control cones keep half of their dark sensitivity.

25
00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:13.080
Cones lacking the sensors keep about 0.110, each measured against its own dark

26
00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:19.640
sensitivity. To give a response of the same size, they need a flash 4.55 times

27
00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:24.440
stronger. These are central fit values from mouse cones, ex vivo.

28
00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:28.960
So the sensors matter, yet the cones still adapt.

29
00:02:29.833 --> 00:02:35.913
The authors read this as a calcium-independent component, and also reported a

30
00:02:35.913 --> 00:02:38.213
further calcium-dependent mechanism.

31
00:02:38.893 --> 00:02:43.333
But a low-calcium bath changes the solution around the retina.

32
00:02:43.833 --> 00:02:49.533
It does not clamp calcium inside the outer segment, and no molecule has been shown

33
00:02:49.533 --> 00:02:51.233
to be the residual mechanism.

34
00:02:51.233 --> 00:02:58.813
So keep this rule: GCAPs and recoverin contribute, and do not account for all of the adaptation.
